{
  "result": "I love mornings before alarms go off, when the house is still dreaming and light tiptoes across the floor in pale gold stripes. Coffee brews.\nOutside, birds rehearse their gossip, squirrels sprint power lines like tightrope walkers, and the air smells faintly of wet soil and possibility and toast. I stand barefoot and listen.\nThere's a quiet freedom in these unwitnessed minutes, no performance, no inbox, just breathing and noticing steam curling from a mug, vanishing like small ghosts.\nI've been thinking lately that attention is love in work clothes. Not fireworks, but remembering how your friend takes tea, or which story your mother repeats.\nLife hides whole novels in sidewalks. Moss fills a crack like a forest. A dog dreams, paws twitching, chasing electric squirrels. Someone's radio leaks nostalgia.\nYesterday the cashier drew a tiny smiley face on my receipt because it was raining. Such a small kindness, completely unprofitable, completely unforgettable. I kept it.\nMaybe meaning isn't found on mountaintops after epic climbs, but collected like sea glass. Piece by piece, smoothed by time, color emerging only after you rinse sand.\nSo today I want to walk slower, listen longer, waste time beautifully on things that will never fit a resume: cloud shapes, sidewalk chalk, distant laughter.\nEventually the house will wake, phones will buzz, lists will demand me. That's fine. For now, this warm mug, this blue almost-morning, this quiet page is enough, more than enough, everything.\nAnd when coffee cools, I'll finally drink, grateful awake alive.",
